A||||30||mblackwell||Rattle can painting advice||||1||
Z||000000||mblackwell||06-06-2001||07:00 PM||||The paint on my hood, decklid, and roof is about 50% worn off.  Almost all of the black primer looks ok, but there are two small chips into the white plastic.  I'm planning on spray painting these areas sometime in the next few weeks.  My plan is to just sand the areas, fix the chips, prime the chiped areas, mask off the surrounding areas, and spray it with cans.  I don't plan to remove any perts, except the spolier.<P>Now for the questions.<BR>1. Will this plan work ok?<BR>2. What should I use to repair the chips?<BR>3. Is duplicolor from wal mart good enough paint?<BR>4. What grit sand paper should I use?<P><BR>I know this won't look as good as a pro job.  I don't have much money, and this is my daily use car, not a show car.  I just want it too look decent.||||reg||1
Z||000001||spideyfoot||06-06-2001||07:11 PM||||Rattle can paint will fade and look like crap before the end of summer, Then you will have a new layer of crap to sand off before you shoot it with real paint. They have no UV protection it would be like printing some decals on your deskjet and putting them on your hood. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||06-06-2001||07:33 PM||||I find Can paint, like duplicolor, that is made for auto applications usually holds up quite well. It's all in how well it's applied.<P>Main thing is to practice before you shoot the car. Runs in primer you can just sand off but not so with paint.<P>Main thing is a number of thin even coats with proper drying time between coats. Follow package instructions. longer dry time is better.<P>The other trick is have the can moving across a patch Before starting to spray. Don't push the button and start moving at the same time.<P>Once you get the base coat down and any overspray buffed off, shoot it with some clear coat. Move the masking away from the patch area before you shoot. You want the clear to caver a bigger area than the base. That will help it blend in better.<P>Once the paint has cured a couple weeks to a month hit it with a good non silicone wax to get teh whole car looing better.||||reg||1
Z||000003||starfighter007@msn.com||06-06-2001||07:48 PM||||hows about goig to your localbody paint store have them mix you up whay ever amount that you think you might need then go to home depot and get one of those jars with the airesol cans on it if the paint shop do not carry these whitch I doubt and do it that way ||||reg||3
Z||000004||84Bill||06-06-2001||08:02 PM||||Upper rear clip, roof and door done with a rattle can4 years ago. fenders hood and lower quarter stock paint. Can you tell?<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/Pictures/image004.jpg">  <P>Oh and the center caps too<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||mblackwell||06-06-2001||08:33 PM||||Looks good, Bill.<P>Ogre, thanks for the advice.<P>Still need to know what to fill the chips in with.<P>I'm gonna try to park in the garage as much as possible to protect it from the sun.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadaver||06-06-2001||08:38 PM||||They make this UV putty. It goes on like regular putty but then you put it in the sun and it hardens. I would fill them with that.||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||06-06-2001||08:43 PM||||Thanks<BR>Here is a thread that explains how to paint a car properly. I worked in a bodyshop for a few years and they had shown me the right way to prep a car. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007781.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007781.html</A> <BR>Be patient when painting on the color, most people using spray cans put way to much on to fast and it runs and chips off in no time. Read and follow the instructions on the can closely as I did and you will have a bodyshop paint job for about 40 or 50 bucks.<BR>Prep the car well as it is the foundation for the paint. Skimp on it and you'll regret it<BR>Good luck Grasshopper  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000008||spideyfoot||06-06-2001||08:54 PM||||Well I guess its the paint you use.  I reccomend if you use a rattle can to set the can in hot water before you shoot. And take care of your tips. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||mblackwell||06-06-2001||09:01 PM||||Thanks!||||reg||7
Z||000010||lowCG||06-06-2001||10:54 PM||||I've had good results with Krylon,am sure Duplicolor is good also.<BR>Chips are best filled with catalyzed spot putty(tack free).Heating the paint is good,doing one panel at a time,to first just mist the panel,then wait for a few minutes(called a tack coat) will allow a better coat with more resistance to sags and runs,also to have at least two full applications,waiting a day or so between coats,wet-sanding lightly with 400 grit before painting again;the paint will flow out way better over a coat of itself than over just primer.To have a "fresh" can to apply the last coat with will help reduce spatter.<BR>After the last coat is on,wait for around a month to let it harden before attempting to polish or wax.<BR>Wear a respirator too!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tweek||06-07-2001||12:21 AM||||How come with rattle can paint I find that If I go for several light coats, the coats come out looking and feeling like sandpaper....   and of course if I put enough on to make it all smooth...   it runs... =(||||reg||1
Z||000012||84Bill||06-07-2001||08:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tweek:<BR><B>How come with rattle can paint I find that If I go for several light coats, the coats come out looking and feeling like sandpaper....   and of course if I put enough on to make it all smooth...   it runs... =(</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Rough finish is ok, just let it cure and sand it out with 400 and then buff it. Viola shine and best of all no runs witch are much harder to get sanded out without removing the paint down to the primer again.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000013||deceler8||06-07-2001||09:04 AM||||My local auto parts store has a machine that mixes paint and puts it in spray cans for a reasonable price ($8 per spray can)...it's the real McCoy.<P>And I used this method for my faded roof and decklid.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/May08$02.jpg"> <P>I did about three light coats immediately followed by clear, then let it all dry dor a few weeks, then wet sanded, cleared again, and then wet sanded with 1500 and then polished out.<P>Can you tell ?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||01:01 PM||||This is some really great advice on painting with spray cans and getting reasonable results.<P>  the thing I have noticed when people go this route is they usually buy one can too few for the job, and it shows in the end.<P>  couple of questions:<P>   when you are spraying, is there a good indication of when you have enough for each coat? do you want to only dust it with each sucessive coat, or should you keep at it till it looks wet?<P>  the other thing I have never heard a good explaination of - the whole wetsanding / buffing procedure.<P>  you let the car dry for, what  - a month, then you wet sand it with 400 or 600 or better<BR>  <BR>  when you are wet sanding it, what do you look for - ie how do you know when to stop or move on? I know how to tell when you have gone too far :-0<P>  and then the buffing - you get a pad for your drill, or you buy a buffer, and then what?<P>  do you use regular turtle wax?<P>  or turtle wax polishing compound?<P> or something else?<P>  do you really lean into it<P>   or buff it really lightly?<P>   and how do you know when you are done with the buffer?<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||01:05 PM||||okay one more question - should you let the base color dry for a month<P>and then wet sand it<P>and then clear coat over it?<P> or can you let the base coat dry for a day<P> and then wet sand it<P>and then clear coat it - and then let the clear coat dry for a month , and then wet sand that , and then buff that out?<P> do you need to wet sand the color coat at all if you are gonna clear coat it?||||reg||1
Z||000016||thomas_l||06-08-2001||01:26 PM||||You can find a good discussion about wet sanding and polishing in <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007720.html" TARGET=_blank>this thread</A>. The finer the grade used on the final sand, as well as keeping the paper clean and wet, will reduce the amount of polishing required.<P>Polishing is as much an art as applying the finish. The tool of choice would be a professional polisher (looks like an angle grinder) running a wool bonnet at 1500-2500 rpm. Grinders are no good because they run too fast, and drills turn too slow. The compound choice is a matter of how aggressive you want to cut and how fast. If the paint is in decent shape, you can use a swirl remover. I had some really bad paint and used 3M Finesse after wet sanding. Generally, you apply fairly strong pressure while the compund is wet and let up as it it dries. Just remember that polishing is actually removing a thin layer of paint so... if you haven't polished before, start with a light touch and fairly mild compound like plain old swirl remover.<P>Before you grind through your own paint, find a junk panel (or car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) to practice on. With the junk panel, scuff it with some 600 or 1000 paper and get the feel for polishing. Last but not least, stay off the corners and ridges with a polisher - do them by hand.||||reg||1
Z||000017||rogergarrison||06-08-2001||01:42 PM||||NO !!! you must clear it while its fresh, I do it withing an hour of last basecoat. DONT wait for more than 24 hours, or you need to spray another coat of basecoat after litely sanding it first.<P>Dont even touch the basecoat with your hand before you clear it. The fingerprints may show in some colors and the oil in your skin will make the clear not stick or fisheye.<P><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>okay one more question - should you let the base color dry for a month<P>and then wet sand it<P>and then clear coat over it?<P> or can you let the base coat dry for a day<P> and then wet sand it<P>and then clear coat it - and then let the clear coat dry for a month , and then wet sand that , and then buff that out?<P> do you need to wet sand the color coat at all if you are gonna clear coat it?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||03:36 PM||||I wet sanded the peeling clear coat off my front hood about a week or two ago<P>and I hit the primer in a few places<P>and found the patch work from where PreviousOwner (PO ?!) hit a deer<P>I was planning on painting the whole car a new color<P>but not for a while<P> now I think you guys have inspired me to redo the hood and the tops of the front fenders in the original silver color with some spray cans and clear coat it, and them buff it out.<P>  if nothing else it will be good practice.<P>  repainting the whole car is on the bottom of my FieroDewList - but this would be a big improvement in the mean-time.||||reg||1
